First Author: Lehr
Title: Extremes of Age Decrease Survival in Adults After Lung Transplant
Source number: 28  ￼

Very short summary: This SRTR study examined how recipient age influences survival after adult lung transplantation using parametric survival modeling and random survival forests. Five key findings were: (1) 14,253 adult lung transplant recipients in the LAS era were included; (2) both the youngest adults (<30 years) and the oldest adults (>55 years) had worse post-transplant survival than recipients aged 30–55 years; (3) age was the most important risk factor for death at all post-transplant time points, and its effect increased over time; (4) random survival forests identified higher creatinine, single-lung transplant, hospitalization before transplant, and higher bilirubin as major mortality predictors in the overall cohort; and (5) age-specific risk patterns differed, with social determinants such as education and insurance disproportionately affecting survival in the youngest recipients. Tables 1–2 and Figures 1–3 were directly used for this extraction.  ￼

