	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: The paper states that “random-survival forest methodology was used to examine risk factors for death,” alongside a nonproportional hazard model. RSF used 96 variables to grow 500 trees, and “final selection of model variables was performed using bootstrap aggregation.” Inputs included recipient, donor, and paired donor-recipient variables. The analysis is better characterized as internal modeling/variable-importance analysis rather than a conventional train-test split or cross-validation framework, so validation is listed as Other.  ￼

