Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: This was a large national SRTR cohort of adult lung transplant recipients in the LAS era, minimizing single-center selection bias.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictor domains included recipient, donor, and donor-recipient matching variables available around listing/transplant, and RSF explicitly evaluated variable interactions with age.  ￼
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Death was ascertained through SRTR linked to the Social Security Death Master File, with patients followed to death or end of follow-up and censored at retransplant.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include national scope, complementary parametric and RSF modeling, bootstrap aggregation, and age-interaction analysis; uncertainty remains because conventional predictive validation metrics such as AUC and calibration were not reported in the retrieved text.  ￼
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The article directly studies machine-learning-assisted risk modeling for post-lung-transplant survival and is clearly relevant to the review question.  ￼

